# MmWaveFlexTtiMacScheduler: the transport-block split aborted the run, and hid
# an underflow the assert could not catch.
#
# ── The abort ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `mmwave-flex-tti-mac-scheduler.cc` divides a DL transport block between a UE's
# active RLC flows with INTEGER division. Enough flows -- which is what enough
# UEs on one narrow-carrier cell produces -- rounds the share to zero, and
#     NS_ASSERT(ueSchedInfo.m_rlcPduInfo[i].m_size > 0)
# aborts ns-3. Reproduced 2026-09-14 on 3 cells / 18 UEs at 10 MHz:
#     NS_ASSERT failed, cond="ueSchedInfo.m_rlcPduInfo[i].m_size > 0",
#     +1.436250000s ... mmwave-flex-tti-mac-scheduler.cc, line=1830
#
# THE TIMESTAMP IS THE POINT. It fires at t=1.44 s, not at startup. The platform
# already guards this with `max_ue_per_cell_for_bw` (engine_envelope.py), but
# that guard is computed from the topology AS DRAWN while the load that trips the
# split arrives later: UEs attach by RSRP, so a cluster concentrates them, and a
# `cell_outage` fault moves a whole cell of traffic onto its neighbours. A static
# guard cannot see a run-time redistribution. A day-long run has a day of chances
# to meet it, which is why this became worth fixing once `kpm_collect_s` made
# long runs possible at all.
#
# ── The defect the assert was hiding ───────────────────────────────────────
# Three lines below the assert:
#     UpdateDlRlcBufferInfo(rnti, lcid, m_rlcPduInfo[i].m_size - m_subHdrSize);
# takes `uint16_t size`, and `m_subHdrSize` is 4. A share of 1..3 bytes therefore
# PASSES `> 0` and underflows to ~65533 -- corrupting RLC buffer accounting
# silently rather than aborting loudly. Nobody had looked for this because the
# assert above it looked like the guard.
#
# ── The fix ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not to relax the assert but to stop producing the value. Serve as many flows as
# the transport block can actually carry at a sub-header plus one byte each, and
# let the rest wait for a later slot. Nothing is dropped: a deferred flow keeps
# its queued bytes and the block still leaves full, spread over fewer flows. The
# assert is then TIGHTENED from `> 0` to `>= m_subHdrSize + 1`, so the underflow
# case is caught too rather than passing silently.
#
# Apply in the engine distro, then rebuild (a full library build, ~minutes --
# this is src/, not scratch):
#   cd /home/builder/ns-O-RAN-flexric/mmwave-LENA-oran
#   patch -p0 < this && runuser -u builder -- ./ns3 build
#
# The tree is configured for the `default` profile, which is the one the product
# loads (run_scenario.sh: NSG_NS3_PROFILE=default). See nsg-ns3-build-profile:
# a rebuild in the wrong profile lands in a library nothing runs.
#
# ── Measured 2026-09-14, nsg-engine:1.16 ───────────────────────────────────
# The abort is gone at both recorded points:
#   3 cells, 18 UE (6/cell), 10 MHz   was: NS_ASSERT at t=1.436 s
#                                     now: clean, span 2.2 s, 40 KPM names, 46 ind
#   3 cells, 30 UE (10/cell), 10 MHz  was: NS_ASSERT
#                                     now: clean, span 1.1 s, 24 indications
#
# And nothing moves in the regime that already worked. Two ways of asking:
#
#   Bare path, per-cell Joules at a FIXED simulated time (t=2.00 s), 20 MHz /
#   6 UEs — BIT-IDENTICAL across the old build, the new build and two repeats:
#       cell2 1331.5 J   cell3 1331.5 J   cell4 2406.7 J
#
#   Product path (real traffic, which is what actually creates multiple RLC
#   flows per UE), same config and seed, three runs per image:
#       DRB.UEThpDl mean   1.15: 12763 / 11978 / 12997    1.16: 12775 / 12126 / 12978
#       RRU.PrbUsedDl mean 1.15: 12.52 / 13.58 / 12.18    1.16: 12.57 / 13.05 / 12.00
#       handovers          1.15: 1 / 1 / 0                1.16: 1 / 1 / 0
#   The two distributions overlap; the difference in means (0.4% throughput,
#   1.7% PRB) is far inside the 8-11% spread BETWEEN RUNS OF THE SAME IMAGE.
#
# A METHOD NOTE worth keeping. The first regression test was a byte-compare of
# /tmp/scn0.log before and after, and it reported DIFFERS at byte 52877 — which
# would have looked like the fix changing results. It does not: two runs of the
# SAME build already differ at byte 6587, because the collection window is bound
# by wall clock and the xApp's subscription lands at a different simulated
# instant each time. Check that a comparison can distinguish anything before
# believing what it says.
#
